%[text] # Stability Analysis of the Mathieu Equation
%[text] [Open in MATLAB Online](https://matlab.mathworks.com/open/github/v1?repo=mxmGrss/phasorArray_Toolbox&file=Exemples/MathieuEquation_Report.m)
%[text] 
%[text] **Abstract**: This report investigates the stability regions of the Mathieu equation using the PhasorArray Toolbox. 
%[text] We compare time-domain simulations with Harmonic State-Space (HSS) eigenvalue computation to visualize stability tongues (Strutt-Ince diagram).
%[text] 
%[text] ## 1. Theoretical Context
%[text] The Mathieu equation is a second-order linear differential equation with periodic coefficients:
%[text] $$$ \\ddot{x} + \[a - 2q \\cos(2t)\] x = 0$$$
%[text] This can be represented as a Linear Time-Periodic (LTP) state-space system $\\dot{x} = A(t)x$, where:
%[text] $A(t) =  \\pmatrix{0 & 1 \\cr -(a - 2q \\cos(2t)) & 0 }$
%[text] 
%[text] ## 2. Implementation
%%
%[text] ### 2.1 Initialization
%[text] We define the fundamental frequency and truncation order for the HSS analysis.
T = pi;             % Period of 2*cos(2t) is pi
omega = 2*pi/T;     % Fundamental frequency (omega = 2)
h_hss = 15;         % Harmonic truncation order
%%
%[text] ### 2.2 Time-Domain Comparison
%[text] We simulate two cases: a stable point ($a=2.0$, $q=0.2$) and an unstable point ($a=1.0$, $q=0.5$).
points = [2.0, 0.2; 1.0, 0.5];
t_sim = linspace(0, 20*T, 1000);
figure(1)
clf %[output:62d7bed4]
for i = 1:2 %[output:group:0fcabdc0]
    a = points(i, 1); q = points(i, 2);
    Ar = PhasorArray([0 1 ; -a 0], [0 0 ; q 0], 'isreal', true);
    sys = PhasorSS(Ar, [0;0], [1 0], 0, T);
    [y, t] = initial(sys, [1; 0], t_sim); %[output:7f840bf2]
    subplot(1, 2, i); %[output:62d7bed4]
    plot(t, squeeze(y), 'LineWidth', 1.5); hold on;
    title(sprintf('Case %d: a=%.1f, q=%.1f', i, a, q));
    grid on;
end %[output:group:0fcabdc0]
%%
%[text] ## 3. Results: HSS Spectrum and Stability Tongues
%[text] ### 3.1 Spectal Analysis
%[text] The eigenvalues of the HSS matrix correspond to the Floquet exponents.
Ar_stable = PhasorArray([0 1 ; -2.0 0], [0 0 ; 0.2 0], 'isreal', true);
ev = Ar_stable.HmqNEig(70, T);
figure(2)
clf %[output:7bb70f0f]
plot(real(ev), imag(ev), 'x'); %[output:7bb70f0f]
title('HSS Spectrum (Stable Case)'); %[output:7bb70f0f]
grid on; %[output:7bb70f0f]
ylim([-1 1]*omega*1) %[output:7bb70f0f]
%%
%[text] ### 3.2 Strutt-Ince Diagram
%[text] We perform a sweep over parameters $a$ and $q$ to map the stability boundaries.
Na = 80; Nq = 80; % Reduced grid for demo
a_range = linspace(-0.5, 4, Na);
q_range = linspace(-1.5, 1.5, Nq);
[A, Q] = meshgrid(a_range, q_range);
Stable = zeros(size(A));
parfor idx = 1:numel(A)
    Ar_scan = PhasorArray([0 1 ; -A(idx) 0], [0 0 ; Q(idx) 0], 'isreal', true);
    Stable(idx) = max(real(Ar_scan.HmqNEig(25, T))) ;
end
%%
figure(3)
clf
imagesc(a_range, q_range, Stable<=1e-6);
set(gca, 'YDir', 'normal'); 
colormap([0.8 0.3 0.3; 0.3 0.8 0.3]);
xlabel('a'); ylabel('q'); title('Stability Tongues');
%%
%[text] ## 4. Conclusion
%[text] The HSS method accurately predicts the stability regions of the Mathieu equation, 
%[text] perfectly matching the numerical time-domain divergence.

